id: unsafe-rendering
type: definition
name: Unsafe Rendering
category: insecure-construction
targets: [source]
related: [weak-cryptography]
tensions: []
rules: [UR-001, UR-002, UR-003]
---

## What It Is

Writing a value into a markup or execution sink that parses it as HTML.
The sink varies — assigning a string to `.innerHTML`/`.outerHTML`,
calling `document.write(...)`, passing `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` in JSX —
but the behaviour is the same: a string becomes live DOM. If any part of
that string is attacker-influenced and unsanitised, the browser will run
injected `<script>`, event handlers, or `javascript:` URLs in the page's
origin. This is the DOM-XSS sink class (CWE-79).

This is a *construction* smell, not a taint finding (ADR-087). The
detector flags that a value is being written to a raw-HTML sink; it does
**not** trace whether that value is actually attacker-controlled or
whether it was sanitised first. A sink fed only by a trusted constant is
safe — the rule surfaces the sink so a human confirms the input contract,
and routes toward `textContent` / a framework-escaped binding / a
sanitiser when the input is dynamic.

## Why It's Harmful

- **Raw-HTML sinks execute markup.** `el.innerHTML = userInput` parses
  the string as HTML. `<img src=x onerror=steal()>` and friends run in
  the page origin — session cookies, DOM, tokens, and any authenticated
  API the page can reach are exposed (CWE-79).
- **`document.write` is doubly dangerous.** Beyond XSS, it can rewrite
  the whole document after load and blocks the parser; the string is
  parsed as HTML with the same injection surface.
- **`dangerouslySetInnerHTML` opts out of React's escaping.** React
  escapes interpolated children by default; this prop is the deliberate
  bypass. It is the single most common way XSS re-enters a React app that
  was otherwise safe by construction.
- **Agents reach for `innerHTML` to "render some HTML".** Asked to
  insert markup, an agent writes `.innerHTML =` because it is the
  shortest path, not because the input is trusted. Catching the sink at
  write-time is the point.

## The Spectrum

- **Warn (severity warning, on by default):** all three sinks — an
  `.innerHTML`/`.outerHTML` assignment (UR-001), `document.write(`
  (UR-002), `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` (UR-003). They warn rather than
  block because a sink fed by a trusted constant or a value already run
  through a sanitiser (DOMPurify) is legitimate; the assumption is that
  most uses are not.
- **Benign (intentionally not flagged):** `textContent` /
  `innerText` assignment (these escape by construction), React's default
  `{value}` child interpolation, a framework template binding, and any
  sink whose value is passed through a sanitiser — the rules name the raw
  sinks only.
- **Known false-positive surface:** the scanner is line-regex based and
  does not strip comments, string bodies, or resolve the value's origin.
  `// el.innerHTML = ...` in a comment, an `.innerHTML =` fed by a static
  literal, or a `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` wrapping a DOMPurify call will
  all trip. Suppress those with the standard directive or the per-file
  allowlist.

## The Right Response

Prefer a sink that does not parse HTML, or escape/sanitise before the
raw sink:

1. **Inserting text** — assign `el.textContent = value` (or
   `el.innerText`). These never parse markup; injected tags render as
   literal text.
2. **Building structure** — create elements
   (`document.createElement` + `append`) and set text/attributes
   individually, so no string is ever parsed as HTML.
3. **You genuinely need rich HTML from dynamic input** — run it through a
   vetted sanitiser (DOMPurify) with an allowlist, immediately before the
   sink, and keep the sanitise-then-assign together so review can see it.
4. **React** — pass the value as a normal escaped child (`{value}`).
   Reserve `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` for sanitiser output only.

If the value is a compile-time constant you control end to end, keep the
sink and suppress the specific rule with a reason noting the input is
static.

## Detection Signals

- **Lexical:** an assignment to `.innerHTML`/`.outerHTML` (`=` or `+=`,
  not `==`/`===`) whose right-hand side is not a quoted string literal
  (UR-001 — like `eval`, it skips `= '...'`/`= "..."`, including the
  `= ''` clearing idiom, but still fires on template literals and
  expressions); a `document.write(`/`document.writeln(` call (UR-002); the
  literal `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` (UR-003).
- **Allowlisted contexts:** test files (`*.test.ts`, `*.spec.ts`,
  `__tests__/`) and `explain/` fixtures opt out — they demonstrate the
  sink intentionally.
- **Scope:** JavaScript/TypeScript extensions only (`.ts`, `.tsx`, `.js`,
  `.jsx`, `.mjs`, `.cjs`) — these sinks are meaningless outside a DOM, and
  `.html`/`.css` are retired from the scan set, so the sinks are caught in
  the script code that assigns them, not in markup files.
- **Negative signals (do not fire):** `.textContent`/`.innerText`
  assignment; equality comparison (`.innerHTML === ...`); React's default
  child interpolation.
- **NOT a negative signal:** comments and string bodies are not filtered
  (line-regex scanner); a sink in a comment or fed by a static literal
  will trip — suppress or rewrite.

## Example

```tsx
// UR-001 trips (raw-HTML sink assignment, dynamic RHS):
el.innerHTML = userProvidedMarkup;        // identifier RHS
row.innerHTML = `<td>${cell}</td>`;       // template literal RHS

// UR-002 trips (document.write):
document.write(location.hash);

// UR-003 trips (React escape bypass):
<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: comment.body }} />;

// Does NOT trip (safe sinks / comparisons / quoted literals):
el.textContent = userProvidedMarkup;      // escapes by construction
if (el.innerHTML === '') resetView();     // comparison, not assignment
container.innerHTML = '';                 // quoted literal (clearing) — skipped like eval
el.innerHTML = '<hr>';                    // static quoted literal — skipped
<div>{comment.body}</div>;                // React escapes by default
```
